I can’t access your internal systems or verify tokens from here. However, I can help you confirm whether the project access token is still active and effectively “rejuvenated” using secure, repeatable checks. Here’s a concise plan and ready-to-run templates you can use in your environment.

What to check to confirm a token is rejuvenated and still valid
- Token introspection: use your OAuth/token introspection endpoint to confirm the token is active and view its metadata (exp, iat, jti, scope, client_id).
- Protected API call: call a known endpoint with the token; a 200 response indicates the token is currently usable for that endpoint.
- Rotation audit: verify you’ve rotated to a new token per policy, check issued-at (iat) or jti against your rotation logs, and ensure old tokens are revoked if applicable.
- Secrets manager parity: ensure all services and deployments pulling the token are updated to the latest one, and that secret versions reflect the new token.
- Logging: keep an audit trail of the verification results without exposing the token in logs.

Ready-to-run templates (replace placeholders with your values)

A) Bash introspection check
#!/usr/bin/env bash
TOKEN="YOUR_TOKEN_HERE"
INTROSPECT_URL="https://internal-api.example.com/oauth2/introspect"

RESP=$(curl -sS -X POST "$INTROSPECT_URL" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded" \
  -d "token=$TOKEN")
echo "$RESP" | jq .

B) Python introspection check
import requests, json

TOKEN = "YOUR_TOKEN_HERE"
INTROSPECT_URL = "https://internal-api.example.com/oauth2/introspect"

resp = requests.post(
    INTROSPECT_URL,
    data={"token": TOKEN},
    headers={"Content-Type": "application/x-www-form-urlencoded"}
)
print(json.dumps(resp.json(), indent=2))

C) Node.js status check
const https = require('https');
const token = 'YOUR_TOKEN_HERE';
const options = {
  hostname: 'internal-api.example.com',
  path: '/v1/status',
  method: 'GET',
  headers: { 'Authorization': `Bearer ${token}` }
};

const req = https.request(options, (res) => {
  console.log(`statusCode: ${res.statusCode}`);
  res.on('data', d => process.stdout.write(d));
});
req.on('error', (e) => console.error(e));
req.end();

Notes
- Replace YOUR_TOKEN_HERE and the URLs with your actual values.
- Do not log the token in plaintext; if you need to debug, redact or mask it.
- If introspection shows active but expiring soon, consider proactive rotation per policy.

If you’d like, tell me:
- Your tech stack (language, endpoints, and which secret manager you use)
- The exact introspection or status endpoint you rely on
- How your token rotation policy is defined (e.g., rotate on deployment, daily, or upon each use)

I can tailor these scripts to your environment and provide a compact runbook or a CI-integrated check to verify token rejuvenation automatically.
